# The Universe and the Solar System: Our Cosmic Address

> Geography begins in space: the Earth is a planet shaped by its
> position around an ordinary star in an ordinary galaxy. This
> lesson tours the universe's structure, the Sun's family, and the
> member worlds nearest to home.

## 1. The Scale of the Universe

[definition]
The **universe** is all of space, time, matter, and energy. Its
largest known structures are galaxies — vast islands of stars — and
our Sun is one of an estimated 100–400 billion stars in the **Milky
Way** galaxy.
[/definition]

| Structure | Description | Our place in it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Universe | Everything; ~13.8 billion years old | — |
| Supercluster | Group of galaxy clusters | Laniakea |
| Cluster / Group | Gravitationally bound galaxies | Local Group |
| Galaxy | Billions of stars, gas, dust | The Milky Way |
| Star system | Star(s) with orbiting bodies | The Solar System |
| Planet | Non-luminous body orbiting a star | Earth (3rd) |

Light itself sets the ruler: light from the Sun takes ~8 minutes 20
seconds to reach Earth, and the Milky Way is about 100,000
**light-years** across — the distance light travels in one year
(about 9.46 trillion km).

[info title="How it all began"]
The **Big Bang** (~13.8 billion years ago) marked the universe's
hot, dense beginning; it has been expanding and cooling ever since —
evidence: the cosmic microwave background and the redshift of
distant galaxies (Hubble, 1929). Galaxies, stars, and planets
condensed from cooling gas over billions of years.
[/info]

## 2. The Solar System: The Sun's Family

The **Sun** holds 99.8% of the solar system's mass; its gravity
governs everything else. Formed ~4.6 billion years ago from a
collapsing cloud of gas and dust, the system contains:

- **8 planets** in near-circular, coplanar orbits
- **Dwarf planets** — Pluto (demoted 2006), Ceres, Eris, Makemake,
  Haumea
- **Moons** orbiting planets (Earth: 1; Jupiter: 95+)
- **Asteroids** — rocky leftovers, mostly between Mars and Jupiter
- **Comets** — icy visitors from the outer dark that grow tails near
  the Sun
- **Meteoroids** — small debris; burning in our atmosphere as
  **meteors** ("shooting stars"); survivors on the ground are
  **meteorites**

### The eight planets, outward from the Sun

| # | Planet | Type | Key fact |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Mercury | Terrestrial | Smallest; fastest orbit (88 days); no atmosphere |
| 2 | Venus | Terrestrial | Hottest (~465 °C) under CO₂ greenhouse; rotates backwards |
| 3 | Earth | Terrestrial | The only known life; 71% ocean; 1 moon |
| 4 | Mars | Terrestrial | Red rust-desert; polar ice; Olympus Mons (largest volcano) |
| 5 | Jupiter | Gas giant | Largest (318 Earth masses); Great Red Spot storm; 95+ moons |
| 6 | Saturn | Gas giant | Spectacular ring system; least dense — would float |
| 7 | Uranus | Ice giant | Rolls on its side (98° tilt); coldest atmosphere |
| 8 | Neptune | Ice giant | Windiest (~2,100 km/h); farthest planet |

