[tip]
Mnemonic for the order: **M**y **V**ery **E**ducated **M**other **J**ust
**S**erved **U**s **N**oodles — Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter,
Saturn, Uranus, Neptune. The **asteroid belt** separates the four
rocky terrestrials from the four giants — a real boundary, not just a
memory aid.
[/tip]

### Why Pluto lost planethood

The 2006 IAU definition requires a planet to (1) orbit the Sun,
(2) be round under its own gravity, and (3) have **cleared its
neighbourhood** of debris. Pluto shares its zone with many
Kuiper-belt objects, so it fails (3) — and is now the king of the
dwarf planets instead.

## 3. The Earth–Moon System

Earth's Moon formed ~4.5 billion years ago, likely from debris of a
Mars-sized impact (the Giant Impact Hypothesis). It:

- Stabilises Earth's axial tilt, keeping climate steadier
- Drives ocean **tides** through its gravity
- Recedes ~3.8 cm/year as tides transfer Earth's spin to the Moon's
  orbit — days slowly lengthen

## 4. Boundaries and Beyond

| Region | Extent | Contents |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Kuiper Belt | 30–50 AU | Icy dwarf planets, comets (Neptune's orbital resonance traps some) |
| Heliopause | ~120 AU | Where solar wind yields to interstellar space — Voyager 1 & 2 have crossed it |
| Oort Cloud | ~2,000–100,000 AU | Spherical shell of pristine cometary nuclei |

1 **AU** (astronomical unit) = average Earth–Sun distance ≈ 149.6
million km. Distances to stars use the **light-year** or
**parsec** (3.26 ly) — Proxima Centauri, the nearest star, is 4.24
light-years away.

## 5. Stars and the Origins of Geography's Raw Materials

The Sun is a **main-sequence star** fusing hydrogen into helium,
roughly halfway through its ~10-billion-year life. Every element
heavier than helium — the iron in Earth's core, the carbon in our
bodies — was forged in earlier stars and scattered by supernovae.
Planets, oceans, and geography itself are recycled stardust.

[summary]
The universe is ~13.8 billion years old and structured in galaxies
within groups, clusters, and superclusters; the Milky Way is our
galaxy, and the solar system sits in its Orion arm. Around the Sun
orbit eight planets — four rocky terrestrials, two gas giants, two
ice giants — divided by the asteroid belt, with the Kuiper Belt,
heliopause, and Oort Cloud marking the frontier. The Moon steadies
Earth's tilt and raises tides, and the Sun's ordinary fusion powers
every geographic process below it.
[/summary]
